{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785699001,"nonce":"pD4lmtnbYsbdB/LumYp3PuhFkEI=","title":"**Verified 2026-08-03. A 20-30s Depot outage during Init DEAD-LETTERS runs today.** This is the prerequisite for Depot HA (git-bug 6cb4a27): HA is pointless if a replica reschedule kills the runs in flight.","message":"**Four things compound:**\n1. **No retry, no timeout, anywhere.** `WorkspaceClient` is a bare `reqwest::Client::new()` (`crates/scarab-workspace-client/src/lib.rs:151`) — no `.timeout()`, no `.connect_timeout()`. `crates/scarab-storage/src/content.rs` contains no retry/backoff/sleep. Every call is one shot.\n2. **The init container is `restart_policy: Never`** (`crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:3782`) so the kubelet never re-runs it — no CrashLoopBackOff, no k8s-side backoff. Commented deliberately at `lib.rs:4544-4546`.\n3. **Fast-fail burns the auto-retry budget in seconds.** connection refused -\u003e `FetchError::Transient` -\u003e exit 1 (`bin/scarab-wsfetch.rs:145`); `workspace_fetch_failed` (`executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:4671`) maps it to `FailureClass::Infra { never_started: true }` (`:4496`, `:4550`); the engine grants `NEVER_STARTED_AUTO_ATTEMPTS = 3` (`scheduler.rs:32, 2789-2791`); exhaustion -\u003e `RunStatus::DeadLettered` (`scheduler.rs:2327-2336`), with a cause blaming \"the step's inputs were never provisioned\".\n4. **A hung Depot has no ceiling but the Pod deadline.** No client timeout means a Depot that accepts TCP and never answers hangs the init container until `activeDeadlineSeconds`, and the step fails as `Timeout` having never run.\n\n**Both clocks run during Init** — the kubelet's `active_deadline_seconds` is anchored at Pod start (`executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:3786`, mirrored by `outlived_deadline` at `:4626-4638`), and the engine backstop at Attempt **claim** time (`scheduler.rs:1645-1652, 2184`), before the Pod exists. `enforce_run_budget` bills it too (`scheduler.rs:2554-2577`). So waiting is not free; it comes out of the build's time. That is acceptable at the 1h default but must be a STATED choice.\n\n**Separate real bug: transient and permanent are the same class.** exit 1 (Depot unreachable) and exit 2 (404, content genuinely gone -\u003e `FetchError::Permanent`, `bin/scarab-wsfetch.rs:300`) BOTH map to `Infra { never_started: true }`. The classifier reads only the exit code and does not distinguish them, so a transient outage is indistinguishable from evicted content — and evicted content should widen the rerun to `clone`, not burn attempts.\n\n**The chosen failure mode (from the HA design): block at step boundaries, never mid-execution, every boundary operation idempotent.** A Depot outage should DELAY steps, not fail them. Note the structural property that makes this safe: with the eager feed, a step Pod does not touch the Depot while it runs — only at Init (fetch) and at the end (drain) — so an outage during a 40-minute build is invisible.\n\n- [ ] Connect + request timeouts on `WorkspaceClient` (there are none)\n- [ ] Bounded retry with backoff in `wsfetch fetch`, sized to cover a replica reschedule (~30-60s), and the same on the drain path\n- [ ] Split classification: connection-class -\u003e transient/retryable; 404 -\u003e permanent, widens the rerun (do NOT burn never-started attempts on it)\n- [ ] Decide and DOCUMENT that Init-phase waiting bills the step budget\n- [ ] A test that a Depot restarted mid-Init delays the step instead of dead-lettering the run\n\n**Related corrections established in the same pass** (not necessarily this ticket's work):\n- The drain misclassification (`classify_drain`, `executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:1913-1919`) is real, but its message ALREADY names \"or the Depot lost its drain records volume\" as a second cause — so the operator is not told nothing. It is still wrong to classify it `FatalConfig`, which allows 1 attempt and ignores the author's `retry:`.\n- **`/have` is warm-only and never asks cold** (`workspaced.rs:1915-1950`, docstring at `:1881-1914`). A fresh replica reports everything missing, so the Pod re-uploads content cold already holds. Wasteful, never wrong — but it means \"new replica\" costs a full re-upload rather than a cache warm.\n- The ledger IS genuinely rebuildable on a re-drive against a replica empty in BOTH ledger and warm (trees are PUT unconditionally, `workspace-client/src/lib.rs:424-425, 473-478`; `/have` reports all blobs missing; the 409 only fires for an existing SUCCESS record). NOT covered: a replica with the ledger but no warm, or vice versa. And self-healing only within the 5-minute `WS_DRAIN_ESCALATION_MS` window.","files":null}]}